# Shell scripts must be LF whatever the checkout platform. Committed or
# checked out with CRLF they do not run on Linux at all: bash reads the
# carriage return as part of the shebang argument and of every command, so
# the first thing that happens is
#
#   smoke-test.sh: line 32: $'\r': command not found
#   smoke-test.sh: line 33: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# The repository's index is already all-LF; these rules pin the working tree
# to match on every platform instead of following each contributor's
# core.autocrlf.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else consumed by a Linux tool rather than by an
# editor. Terraform itself is indifferent, but a mixed-ending file makes every
# diff after a `terraform fmt` on the other platform unreadable.
*.tf text eol=lf
*.tftest.hcl text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Markdown too: the READMEs are generated in part by terraform-docs, which
# writes LF, and `terraform-docs --output-check` compares its LF output
# against the file on disk. Without this line git rewrites those files to
# CRLF on a Windows checkout, so the docs check reports stale files whose
# committed bytes are identical, and "fixing" it commits line-ending churn
# with no visible change. eol=lf rather than plain `text` because it has to
# win on every platform, not follow core.autocrlf.
#
# After adding a rule here, run `git add --renormalize .` once so the index
# matches; existing checkouts otherwise keep the endings they were cloned
# with.
*.md text eol=lf
